'''Aer''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|aɪər}} {{respell|YRE}}) is an [[Indo-Aryan language]] spoken by around 100 people in [[Sindh]], [[Pakistan]] and [[Gujarat]], [[India]].<ref name=e25/> It is one of the smallest languages of Pakistan in terms of number of speakers,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Torwali |first=Zubair |date=28 July 2015 |title=Saving local languages |url=https://www.thenews.com.pk/print/53367-saving-local-languages |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=[[The News International]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Arshad |first=Muhammad |date=2021-09-06 |title=How Many Languages Are Spoken In Pakistan |url=https://www.economy.pk/how-many-languages-are-spoken-in-pakistan/ |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=Economy.pk |language=en-US}}</ref> and is spoken in rural areas of Sindh including [[Hyderabad, Sindh|Hyderabad]], [[Kot Ghulam Muhammad Tehsil|Kot Ghulam Muhammad]], and [[Kunri Tehsil|Kunri]]. Some speakers are also reported to be living in [[Shaheed Benazirabad District|Shaheed Benazirabad]], but they have a different culture.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Parekh |first=Rauf |date=2018-01-02 |title=Some endangered Pakistani languages |url=https://www.dawn.com/news/1380191 |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=[[Dawn (newspaper)|Dawn]] |language=en}}</ref>

== History ==
After the [[Partition of India]] in 1947, the [[Muslims|Muslim]] speakers of the language migrated to Pakistan, while the [[Hindus|Hindu]] speakers stayed in India. Most of the Aer speakers in Pakistan live in Deh 333,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Rahman |first=Tariq |date=January 2004 |title=Language Policy and Localization in Pakistan: Proposal for a Paradigmatic Shif |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2937498_Language_Policy_and_Localization_in_Pakistan_Proposal_for_a_Paradigmatic_Shift |website=ResearchGate }}</ref> near [[Hyderabad, Sindh|Hyderabad]] in lower Sindh. The language was formed in the [[Indian subcontinent]] during the [[Delhi Sultanate]] and [[Mughal Empire]] due to the influence of [[Persian language|Persian]], [[Arabic]], and [[Turkish language|Turkish]] on Indian languages.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bukhari |first=Syed Muhammad Azeem Shah |date=7 April 2019 |title=معدوم ہوتی پاکستانی زبانیں |trans-title=Endangered Pakistani languages |url=https://www.express.pk/story/1620848/1/ |access-date=31 August 2022 |website=[[Express News (TV channel)|Express News]]}}</ref>

A 1998 estimate concluded there were around 200 native speakers.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Did you know Aer is endangered? |url=https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/lang/3596 |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=Endangered Languages |language=en}}</ref> Current estimates, however, put the number of speakers at 150, further cementing its 'endangered' status.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Parekh |first=Rauf |date=2017-03-13 |title=Literary notes: How many languages are spoken in Pakistan? |url=https://www.dawn.com/news/1320097 |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=[[Dawn (newspaper)|Dawn]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Classification ==
Aer has been classified as one of the [[Gujarati language|Gujarati]] languages. [[Ethnologue]] reports that the closest language is [[Kachi Koli]] and especially its dialects [[Katai Meghwar]] and [[Kachi Bhil]],<ref name="e25"/> and that most Aer speakers are bilingual in [[Sindhi language|Sindhi]]. Two varieties of Aer are Jikrio Goth Aer and Jamesabad Aer.<ref name=":1" />

== Alphabet ==
Aer is commonly not written, but when it is, it is written in a variety of the [[Arabic script]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=ScriptSource - Entry - Aer language keyboard |url=https://www.scriptsource.org/cms/scripts/page.php?item_id=entry_detail&uid=dy2kh5uv3e |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=www.scriptsource.org}}</ref>

